  "profile": {
    "video_id": "iron-snail:w3nib0_vlfk",
    "purpose": "A beginner-to-enthusiast guide to raw Japanese selvedge denim, meant as the first entry in a planned raw selvedge denim series. It covers what raw and selvedge denim are, how shuttle looms work, key denim vocabulary, durability, sizing, and why Japanese selvedge is expensive and prized.",
    "video_type": "explainer",
    "thesis": "Raw and selvedge denim are two different things. Selvedge comes from slow, inefficient shuttle looms that produce a self-finished edge. Japanese selvedge grew out of mills obsessively reproducing vintage Levi's denim. Its cost and appeal come from slow half-width weaving, low-processed character yarns, and logistically complex small runs full of details.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "Raw denim is any denim not yet washed by you or the manufacturer. Selvedge denim is a self-finished fabric made on a shuttle loom; the two are often paired but are separate concepts.",
      "On a shuttle loom the weft yarn loops back and forth instead of being cut, which creates the self-edge. Air-jet and projectile looms shoot cut weft yarns and need the edges closed.",
      "Early shuttles were threaded by mouth and shared around factories, which the video links to the spread of disease (said as typhoid and corrected once to tuberculosis).",
      "Vocabulary covered: slub, nep, chain stitch (Union Special 43200G and roping), vintage vs high-contrast fades, whiskers, honeycombs, train tracks and stacking.",
      "On durability: short-staple cotton, low-tension weaving, slubs, neps and vegetable matter can weaken the fabric, but heavier weights (14–16 oz+) offset this. Many selvedge brands such as Iron Heart use strong, consistent cottons.",
      "Sizing advice: go about one size up in the waist and roughly 3.5–4% longer in length to allow for shrinkage, then hem later, ideally with a Union Special chain stitch.",
      "History: post-WWII American GIs popularised Levi's in Japan, and Osaka mills replicated the denim. Levi's lawsuits pushed repro brands to remix details such as patches, arcuates and tabs with Japanese influences.",
      "Cost drivers: half-width, slow shuttle-loom denim (around 4x regular denim cost), ethical labour and water treatment, plus logistically complex small production runs with many special threads, rivets and patches."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "Intro",
        "summary": "Shows reproductions of vintage Levi's denim and explains why Japanese collectors demand exact recreations. Teases the shuttle-loom disease story and showcases textured, strong denims.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 2
      },
      {
        "title": "Agenda",
        "summary": "Introduces the ultimate raw Japanese selvedge denim guide and the larger planned series. Lays out the agenda: definitions, vocabulary, durability, washing, fit, and cost/specialness.",
        "first_segment": 2,
        "last_segment": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "Raw vs Selvedge",
        "summary": "Distinguishes raw denim (unwashed, possibly sanforized or skewed; loom state denim leg twist) from selvedge denim. Explains the self-finished edge, the selvedge ID and the shuttle loom's inefficiencies.",
        "first_segment": 3,
        "last_segment": 6
      },
      {
        "title": "Weaving",
        "summary": "Explains warp, weft, shed and reed, then contrasts shuttle looms (playing catch) with modern projectile and air-jet looms. Shows why weft looping creates a self edge, and tells the story of mouth-threaded shuttles spreading disease.",
        "first_segment": 7,
        "last_segment": 11
      },
      {
        "title": "Vocabulary",
        "summary": "Rapid-fire denim terms: slub, nep, chain stitch and the Union Special 43200G, roping, vintage vs high-contrast fades, whiskers, honeycombs, train tracks and stacking.",
        "first_segment": 13,
        "last_segment": 15
      },
      {
        "title": "Durability",
        "summary": "Asks whether selvedge denim is stronger than regular denim. Covers short-staple cotton, low-tension weaving, slubs, neps and vegetable matter as weaknesses, offset by heavier fabric weights and stronger cottons used by brands like Iron Heart.",
        "first_segment": 17,
        "last_segment": 20
      },
      {
        "title": "Sizing",
        "summary": "Recommends sizing up slightly in the waist and buying 3.5–4% longer to allow for shrinkage, then hemming later with a Union Special machine, e.g. at Railcar.",
        "first_segment": 20,
        "last_segment": 21
      },
      {
        "title": "Why is it so expensive",
        "summary": "Traces post-WWII Levi's popularity in Japan, Osaka mills replicating denim, and Levi's lawsuits leading to remixed details with Japanese influences. Breaks down costs from half-width shuttle-loom denim, ethical production and complex small-run logistics.",
        "first_segment": 22,
        "last_segment": 27
      }
    ],
